.TH REMQUO 3 "2026-05-06" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
remquo, remquof, remquol \- remainder with quotient bits
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double remquo(double " x ", double " y ", int *" quo );
.br
.BI "float remquof(float " x ", float " y ", int *" quo );
.br
.BI "long double remquol(long double " x ", long double " y ", int *" quo );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR remquo ()
function computes the IEEE 754 remainder of the division of
.I x
by
.I y
(same as
.BR remainder (3)),
and stores a copy of the low-order bits of the quotient (along with the
sign) in the
.B int
variable pointed to by
.IR quo .
.PP
Specifically,
.I *quo
is set to
.I sign(x/y) * (quotient & 0x7) ,
where the quotient is computed using IEEE 754 rounding (round-to-nearest,
ties to even).  The sign of
.I *quo
is the sign of
.I x/y .
.PP
This function is useful for argument reduction in trigonometric functions
and other algorithms that require both the remainder and knowledge of which
octant or quadrant the original quotient falls into.
.PP
The
.BR remquof ()
and
.BR remquol ()
variants take and return
.B float
and
.B "long double"
values respectively.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR remquo ()
returns the remainder of the division (the same value as
.BR remainder (3)),
and stores the quotient bits in
.RI * quo .
.PP
If
.I y
is zero, the function returns NaN and stores an unspecified value in
.RI * quo .
.PP
If
.I x
or
.I y
is NaN, NaN is returned and
.RI * quo
is set to 0.
.PP
If
.I x
is \(+/-\(infinity, NaN is returned and
.RI * quo
is set to 0.
.PP
If
.I y
is \(+/-\(infinity and
.I x
is finite, the remainder (
.I x
itself) is returned and
.RI * quo
is set to
.I sign(x/y) .
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIy\fR is zero
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR remquo (),
.BR remquof (),
.BR remquol ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR remquo ()
and its variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The handling of special arguments follows C99 Annex F (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program uses
.BR remquo ()
to implement trigonometric argument reduction for sine:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double angle = 5.0 * M_PI;
    int quo;
    double remainder = remquo(angle, M_PI, &quo);

    printf("Angle: %.4f radians\en", angle);
    printf("Remainder: %.4f, Quadrant bits: %d\en", remainder, quo);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
